INT. AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL TOWER, REGIONAL AIRPORT - NIGHT

Radar sweeps in green. A single window overlooks a dark runway edged with blue lights. Coffee cups, sticky notes. The storm outside rattles the glass.

CHIEF CONTROLLER BEATRICE "BEA" LINDGREN-ADEBAYO (50s, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain) sits at the main console, headset on. Beside her, TRAINEE GABRIEL "GABE" OKONKWO-DELACROIX (20s, nervous, tie loosened) watches the radar. In the doorway, SUPERVISOR HAROLD "HAL" PEMBERTON-NAKAMURA (60s, coat still wet) shakes rain from his hat.

HAL
Status, Bea.

BEA
One inbound. Cessna, tail number November-seven-two-one-Papa. Low fuel, low visibility.

HAL
And you're talking to him?

BEA
I've been talking to him for eleven minutes.

GABE
Chief, he's off the glide path.

BEA
I see it, Gabe.

PILOT (V.O.)
(over the radio, calm but thin)
Tower, November-seven-two-one-Papa. I can't see the runway.

BEA
Seven-two-one-Papa, Tower. Understand. Turn left heading two-seven-zero. I'll talk you in.

PILOT (V.O.)
Left two-seven-zero. Tower, I'm down to ten minutes of fuel.

HAL
(low)
Bea. Is there an alternate?

BEA
Forty miles. He won't make it.

GABE
We could light the road. Flares along the perimeter.

HAL
Do it.

GABE
Yes, sir.

Gabe grabs a radio, bolts out. The door slams. Bea watches the screen, unblinking.

BEA
Seven-two-one-Papa, Tower. What's your name?

PILOT (V.O.)
Tower?

BEA
Your name, sir.

PILOT (V.O.)
Daniel. Dan Eriksen.

BEA
Dan, I'm Bea. We're going to get you home.

PILOT (V.O.)
Thank you, Bea.

HAL
(muttering)
Come on. Come on.

BEA
Dan, you're two miles out. Descend slowly. Five hundred feet.

PILOT (V.O.)
Five hundred. I see something. Fire. Orange lights on the ground.

BEA
Those are flares. Follow them.

PILOT (V.O.)
Following.

Hal steps up beside her. Puts a hand on her shoulder. She doesn't turn.

BEA (CONT'D)
One mile, Dan. Gear down.

PILOT (V.O.)
Gear down and locked.

BEA
You're over the fence. Cut power.

A long silence. Static. The storm.

PILOT (V.O.)
(half-laughing, half-sobbing)
Tower, Seven-two-one-Papa. On the ground.

Bea exhales. Hal's hand tightens on her shoulder, then lets go. Down the stairs, Gabe's boots pound back up.

HAL
Good work, Chief.

BEA
(removing her headset)
Don't call me Chief. I'm going home to my cat.
